{"kind":"event","event_id":4554,"event_uid":"01M2K7EPAAHY02AYYKPDG3C5DR","origin_instance_uid":"01M29GPTT4YE7ZYSCW8FVMKZ1J","type":"issue.commented","project_id":31,"project_uid":"01M2HBYF26YQWA0S07E1J3NSGF","project_name":"popmechanic-ultrapowers","issue_id":216,"issue_uid":"01M2K7CQVHTA4MBYXR2S136A4X","issue_short_id":"6a4x","actor":"exam:1@run-152","hlc_physical_ms":1789499234634,"hlc_counter":0,"content_hash":"ca1eb05acbb8c75d7e4b3ece94865f52b5a7afd3141c25159ab9537ca338b9c3","payload":{"comment_uid":"01M2K7EPA8R6NWCMVFAX8P2CAW","author":"exam:1@run-152","body":"exam-1: writing tests/test_fold_wave_anchor.py. Approach: a pytest file that builds a temp git repo with git subprocesses (BASE commit a.txt=l1..l8 + b.txt, head H1 on top), captures a task patch with git diff from a BASE worktree, and drives skills/ultrapowers/kernel/fold_wave.py's fold/resolve/materialize as subprocesses. One test per Proof leg (a..g), each named for its Machine clause. For leg (b) I will freeze the BASE kernel's reading of a two-task same-anchor wave as literals computed by running the BASE kernel now, before any edit.","created_at":"2026-09-15T19:07:14.632802059Z"},"created_at":"2026-09-15T19:07:14.634Z"}
{"kind":"event","event_id":4555,"event_uid":"01M2K7J4DQYW48QJYYZ9TWMFB9","origin_instance_uid":"01M29GPTT4YE7ZYSCW8FVMKZ1J","type":"issue.commented","project_id":31,"project_uid":"01M2HBYF26YQWA0S07E1J3NSGF","project_name":"popmechanic-ultrapowers","issue_id":216,"issue_uid":"01M2K7CQVHTA4MBYXR2S136A4X","issue_short_id":"6a4x","actor":"impl:1@run-152","hlc_physical_ms":1789499347383,"hlc_counter":0,"content_hash":"72a6a0fed9b34c6557e2fa6aedcd2ed011778c2331c0ae44c477a7b24efdff89","payload":{"comment_uid":"01M2K7J4DNADVB7B1F4VSP8RXB","author":"impl:1@run-152","body":"Approach for task 1 (anchored patch fold):\n- TaskRef gains a 4th field `anchor` (None = the wave's --base); `_parse_patch` splits an `@<sha>` suffix (last @, only when the left side is an existing file, so a patch path containing @ still parses).\n- `_resolve_tasks` rev-parses the anchor, drops it to None when it equals --base (so the same-anchor path is byte-identical), applies the patch over `anchor or base` and names the anchor in the does-not-apply refusal.\n- `repo_weave.TaskState` gains `anchor_base` (a RepoState, default None). `_fold_text` re-roots the frontier's weave over the anchor's file (`update_state(anchor_base.files[p], current_lines(files[p]))`) before `merge_states`, which makes the merge a three-way over the anchor; `_text_kind`/`_drop_superseded_text`/`_fold_presence` read the anchor base for an anchored task.\n- The frontier base stays the --base snapshot, scoped to the union of each task's OWN touched set (`diff_paths(anchor_i, tree_i)`) — new helper `frontier_fold.union_touched_anchored`.\n- The fold event gains `anchor` only when it differs from the log's base; `rehydrate` reads `e.get('anchor', base)` for both apply_patch_tree and publish. materialize derives its touched set per fold event against that event's anchor.\n- The weave shadow declines an anchored wave (it re-publishes over --base by construction); it only ever writes the sidecar.","created_at":"2026-09-15T19:09:07.381632289Z"},"created_at":"2026-09-15T19:09:07.383Z"}
{"kind":"event","event_id":4556,"event_uid":"01M2K8592J2ACA4M0N6RR44K0H","origin_instance_uid":"01M29GPTT4YE7ZYSCW8FVMKZ1J","type":"issue.commented","project_id":31,"project_uid":"01M2HBYF26YQWA0S07E1J3NSGF","project_name":"popmechanic-ultrapowers","issue_id":216,"issue_uid":"01M2K7CQVHTA4MBYXR2S136A4X","issue_short_id":"6a4x","actor":"exam:1@run-152","hlc_physical_ms":1789499974738,"hlc_counter":0,"content_hash":"e4575ba32ffb92481507f8a3a56600a26ec8771086277274553d385baf0a0886","payload":{"comment_uid":"01M2K8592G8PBMBJMHZ7T2N63Y","author":"exam:1@run-152","body":"exam-1: tests/test_fold_wave_anchor.py is in, 13 tests, red at BASE (10 failed / 3 passed) with every failure reading `--patch t=p.patch@<sha>: no such file` — the spec form does not parse yet. Verified satisfiable: with a throwaway anchor implementation in the tree (anchor on TaskRef, @-suffix split in _parse_patch, per-task anchor publish in _prepare, the anchor-base re-root in rw.fold, e.get('anchor') in rehydrate, per-task touched in cmd_materialize) all 13 pass; the kernel was then restored to BASE and only the exam is committed.\n\nReadings a later session would otherwise have to reconstruct:\n\n1. M1 leg (a) needs a head whose edit breaks `git apply` but not the weave merge. `git apply --cached` does NOT check the index blob sha (measured), so an H1 that only moves l8 still takes the l2 patch cleanly — the leg would not discriminate. The exam uses H1 = l4→L4: l4 sits inside the l2 hunk's 3-line context, so apply fails over H1, while the re-rooted three-way of l2-vs-l4 folds clean (measured against repo_weave directly). l3→L3 would NOT do: adjacent lines conflict.\n\n2. M2 leg (b)'s frozen BASE reading, measured on base=BASE commit, task u = l2→U2, task v = l2→V2, both captured against that commit, fixture a.txt = l1..l8 + b.txt = b1/b2: fold triples ('fold','u','4ca74fce06ca464223c904948dadacbd3c0dae10') and ('fold','v','0df389f04892a974a5380d1b93afb6627318ff3a'); one dispatchable `lines` conflict on a.txt at epoch 2 with hunkCount 1; reply h1.txt = 'U2\\nV2\\n' → candidate a.txt = 'l1\\nU2\\nV2\\nl3..l8\\n'. conflicts.json is NOT byte-stable as written: `hunksFile` is an absolute wave-dir path, so the exam compares its bytes with the wave dir replaced by `<WAVE-DIR>` — that is the only normalization.\n\n3. M7's vendor leg cannot use the Proof's `git diff --quiet $ULTRA_BASE` inside a committed exam, so it pins the tracked vendor file list (PROVENANCE.md, manyana.py) plus a sha256 over their bytes, 70139b6a7c9e322ba6ade1574fe82c498fca6ce2a8c641fde83fb6824e90f819. __pycache__ is deliberately out of scope — it is untracked and regenerated, so a digest over the directory would be unstable.\n\n4. Leg (d)'s 'a second materialize returns the same candidateSha' only holds with a pinned clock: commit-tree stamps the candidate. The exam pins GIT_AUTHOR_DATE/GIT_COMMITTER_DATE and the identity for every subprocess, so that leg is a statement about the fold and not about the second.\n\n5. Leg (e)'s delete leg folds clean: the task's b.txt delete over its anchor, against a head that never touched b.txt, yields no conflict and drops b.txt from the manifest (measured). So the exam is entitled to assert complete=true before materializing.\n\nNothing is unsatisfiable; no leg went unencoded.","created_at":"2026-09-15T19:19:34.736061620Z"},"created_at":"2026-09-15T19:19:34.738Z"}
