{"kind":"event","event_id":4566,"event_uid":"01M2K9642JMR08FA95WW63MXN9","origin_instance_uid":"01M29GPTT4YE7ZYSCW8FVMKZ1J","type":"issue.commented","project_id":31,"project_uid":"01M2HBYF26YQWA0S07E1J3NSGF","project_name":"popmechanic-ultrapowers","issue_id":216,"issue_uid":"01M2K7CQVHTA4MBYXR2S136A4X","issue_short_id":"6a4x","actor":"engine:run-152","hlc_physical_ms":1789501050962,"hlc_counter":0,"content_hash":"589b54abefd587b8654e7eeb25b4e2ef755f7fb6ed2781d10a3af20ef529f8b8","payload":{"comment_uid":"01M2K9642G4G6QJ4PJ26RRZ509","author":"engine:run-152","body":"{\"kind\":\"driver:amendment\",\"task\":\"1\",\"amends\":\"clause\",\"what\":\"The Context's re-root sentence — \\\"the frontier's current lines are re-stated as an edit over the anchor's file, one step before merge_states\\\" — is implemented in the base snapshot instead of inside the fold. `rw.chained_snapshots` publishes the wave's base over the anchors (per path: oldest anchor is initial_state, each later anchor and then the base are update_state of the previous), so the frontier and an anchored task already meet at the anchor's file; `_fold_text` calls `merge_states` exactly as it did at BASE.\",\"why\":\"Implemented literally, the re-root breaks a wave that mixes anchored and base-anchored tasks. Re-rooting the frontier rewrites it to descend from the anchor, while a base-anchored task's weave still descends from the base snapshot; the two then share no ancestry and `merge_states` reads disjoint edits as a conflict. A three-task wave (two anchored at BASE editing lines 1 and 10, one anchored at `--base` editing line 3, all disjoint) lost all three edits and narrated a spurious conflict, and materialize fell back. It is also order-dependent, which K1 would report as exit 3 — except the conflict path returns before self-checks run. Chaining the snapshot gives every weave in the wave one root, so the anchored merge is the three-way over the anchor by construction and the mixed wave folds clean in every task order. Every machine clause M1–M7 reads the same; M2's byte-identity against the BASE kernel is unaffected (with no anchors the chain is exactly `snapshot_scoped`).\",\"id\":\"01M2K9641A00004DTNGCFVF66V\",\"ts\":1789501050922}","created_at":"2026-09-15T19:37:30.960701640Z"},"created_at":"2026-09-15T19:37:30.962Z"}
